We begin by drawing a little picture. The hexagon is given by \begin{align*}
  R &= \left\lbrace x + iy \ | \ -1/2 \leq x < 1/2, -\sqrt{3} \leq y < \sqrt{3} \right\rbrace. \\
  S &= \left\lbrace \frac{1}{x+iy} \ | \ x+iy \in R \right\rbrace, 
\end{align*}which we rewrite as \begin{align*}
  S &= \left\lbrace \frac{1}{x^2 + y^2} (x-iy) \ | \ x+iy \in R \right\rbrace. \\
\end{align*}It is clear that if $x \in R$, then $\frac{1}{x} \notin R,$ so every point in $S$ is outside the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin.  Further, for any $r > 1$, the union of the circles of radius $r$ centered at \begin{align*}
  a &= \left( \frac{1}{2}, \pm \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right), \\
  b &= \left( -\frac{1}{2}, \pm \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right), \\
  c &= \left( 0, \pm \sqrt{3} \right),
\end{align*} is contained in $R;$ in particular, these circles intersect, so the union of these circles contains the equilateral triangle with side length one, which is contained in $R$. The set $S$ is the union of the interiors of these circles, so the area of $S$ is the area of the circles minus the area of the equilateral triangle, both of which can be easily evaluated. The area of $S$ is just the area of the six circles minus the area of one equilateral triangle with side length one, which is $6(\pi - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4})$.\end{think} <answer> $6\pi -\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}$ </answer>

